WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.457
(soft music)

00:00:05.094 --> 00:00:08.014
- I've been working with marine
mammals for almost 40 years.

00:00:08.014 --> 00:00:09.011
Come on up!

00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:10.019
Good girl.

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:11.160
When I started working
with seals and sea lions,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.579
good job kiddo,

00:00:12.579 --> 00:00:14.899
I was under the impression
that the trainer

00:00:14.899 --> 00:00:17.016
was supposed to be in
charge of the session,

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:18.349
not the animals.

00:00:21.037 --> 00:00:22.381
Good boy!

00:00:22.381 --> 00:00:24.355
That's how most aquariums do it

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:26.077
but I don't think that way anymore.

00:00:26.077 --> 00:00:27.219
Good!

00:00:27.219 --> 00:00:29.636
(soft music)

00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:36.979
- [Narrator ] Welcome to
the New England Aquarium.

00:00:36.979 --> 00:00:39.517
Here we have a selection
of its million or so

00:00:39.517 --> 00:00:40.850
annual visitors.

00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:44.834
And here we have Myrtle the Turtle.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.821
But we're really here to
introduce you to Kathy Streeter.

00:00:49.821 --> 00:00:52.701
Some people call her the seal whisperer

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:56.040
and this is her marine mammal team.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.717
- Here at the aquarium
we have six harbor seals,

00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:02.384
three sea lions and
four northern fur seals.

00:01:03.779 --> 00:01:06.781
Some of the animals were rescued

00:01:06.781 --> 00:01:09.277
and some of them were born here.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:10.280
Ready?

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.113
Good!

00:01:12.378 --> 00:01:13.757
- [Narrator] Like most aquarium trainers,

00:01:13.757 --> 00:01:18.120
Kathy's team trains
their animals with food.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:19.197
For example.

00:01:19.197 --> 00:01:22.077
- We taught a harbor seal to
mimic the sound of our voice.

00:01:22.077 --> 00:01:23.880
He can utter how are you?

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:24.877
- How are you?

00:01:24.877 --> 00:01:25.882
- How are you?

00:01:25.882 --> 00:01:29.539
- [Narrator] But the team
does something that most

00:01:29.539 --> 00:01:30.557
aquariums don't:

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:33.718
they also interact with
their animals without food.

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:35.275
- We call it play.

00:01:35.275 --> 00:01:39.240
So instead of us spending
the day dictating throughout

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.581
what we want them to do,

00:01:41.581 --> 00:01:43.832
we are encouraging them to show us

00:01:43.832 --> 00:01:45.837
what they would like us to do.

00:01:45.837 --> 00:01:48.477
(soft music)

00:01:48.477 --> 00:01:50.061
It's totally up to them.

00:01:50.061 --> 00:01:51.421
- [Narrator] Tic tac toe?

00:01:51.421 --> 00:01:53.720
- Right now we're teaching
her the strategy of

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.114
just going to the corners.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:56.537
Good.

00:01:56.537 --> 00:01:58.019
Where you gonna go?

00:01:58.019 --> 00:01:59.661
Oh no, come here.

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:00.494
Yeah?

00:02:01.437 --> 00:02:02.440
- [Narrator] Mirror mirror.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.797
(soft music)

00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:06.659
String on a stick.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:09.535
- Luna loves to play
with string type things.

00:02:09.535 --> 00:02:10.957
Good girl.

00:02:10.957 --> 00:02:13.501
Ultimately I feel like it really
enriches the animals' lives

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:17.880
to be able to participate
with us in a whole different

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.861
type of interaction and
one that is just not based

00:02:20.861 --> 00:02:22.359
on food.

00:02:22.359 --> 00:02:24.440
So we use that as the
reinforcement instead of fish

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:28.061
and in Sierra's session
where she was doing the

00:02:28.061 --> 00:02:30.477
initial stages of learning
how to play tic tac toe,

00:02:30.477 --> 00:02:32.760
at some point she started to vocalize.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.927
So she offered to do
vocalizations instead of doing

00:02:38.556 --> 00:02:40.424
the tic tac toe.

00:02:40.424 --> 00:02:42.099
So we learned through play,

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:43.959
what are their preferences.

00:02:43.959 --> 00:02:46.376
(soft music)

00:02:47.997 --> 00:02:50.760
The standard comment we
get from trainers that

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:53.939
visit from other aquariums
is that they're amazed

00:02:53.939 --> 00:02:56.840
that we can spend time out
on exhibit with our seals

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:00.317
and sea lions without
having food to interact

00:03:00.317 --> 00:03:01.320
with them.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.699
Everybody would love
to be able to do that.

00:03:03.699 --> 00:03:07.000
I think that play behavior for children,

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.819
for adults, and for animals
is all extremely valuable

00:03:10.819 --> 00:03:12.118
in the same regards.

00:03:12.118 --> 00:03:14.243
It just expands your brain.

00:03:14.243 --> 00:03:15.597
(laughs)

00:03:15.597 --> 00:03:18.014
(soft music)

